        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(signal): add setup wizard container flow",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a problem where Signal setup required users to hand-configure native `signal-cli` even when they wanted OpenClaw to guide them through a local Signal REST container or an existing Signal API server. It also fixes setup/status gaps where Signal transport mode, receive readiness, and multi-account setup state could be misreported or persisted on the wrong account.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSignal setup now owns native, Docker container, and existing-server setup paths inside the Signal plugin, while core setup gains only the generic `setupApplied` rollback signal needed to avoid persisting failed or cancelled setup attempts. The runtime can distinguish native and container Signal APIs, probe receive readiness, and persist per-account transport settings without moving Signal-specific policy into core.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers can configure Signal through the setup wizard with a Docker container flow, QR/linking guidance, and existing-server reconfiguration that does not require re-entering an already saved Signal number. Operators get clearer `channels status` output for ready, account-missing, receive-unavailable, and unreachable Signal states, and mixed multi-account configs keep transport fields scoped to the intended account.\n\n## Evidence\n\nBehavioral proof:\n\n- Non-interactive existing-server setup now resets stale Docker transport state to `apiMode: \"auto\"` and `autoStart: false`, so a previous container setup does not force a later `--http-url` config through the container adapter.\n- Non-interactive native setup now resets stale Docker transport state to `apiMode: \"native\"`, `autoStart: true`, and clears `httpUrl`, so a later `--cli-path`/host/port setup does not keep using an old external server.\n- Failed or cancelled setup returns `setupApplied: false`, so core setup skips account selection hooks, setup callbacks, completion notes, and partial config persistence for unsuccessful setup attempts.\n- Signal status/probe output now exposes readiness-specific states instead of collapsing account-missing or receive-unavailable probes into generic working/unreachable output.\n- Screenshots are omitted because this PR changes terminal/setup wizard and gateway status behavior; the reviewer-checkable proof is the command/test output and config transition coverage below.\n\nAutomated checks run on the final target:\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/signal/src/core.test.ts extensions/signal/src/setup-core.test.ts` passed: 2 files, 106 tests.\n- `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.extensions.json --incremental --tsBuildInfoFile .artifacts/tsgo-cache/extensions.tsbuildinfo` passed.\n- `pnpm config:docs:check` passed.\n- `node --import tsx scripts/generate-bundled-channel-config-metadata.ts --check` passed.\n- `node --max-old-space-size=8192 --import tsx scripts/generate-plugin-sdk-api-baseline.ts --check` passed.\n- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` passed.\n- Native review phase: `codex review --base origin/main` returned clean on the final target.\n- Cold review phase: one independent cold review of `origin/main...HEAD` returned no actionable findings on the same final target.\n\nSafety: this proof did not touch real `~/.openclaw/openclaw.json`, did not send Signal messages, and did not start arbitrary OpenClaw agents.\n",
